WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.571
A monotonic stack finds, for every item, the next item that is bigger or smaller, in one pass.

00:00:07.921 --> 00:00:11.439
Picture people standing in a line at a parade, looking right.

00:00:11.889 --> 00:00:16.103
Short people behind a tall person can never be seen past that tall person.

00:00:16.553 --> 00:00:22.010
So when a tall person arrives, every shorter person waiting has found their answer, and they leave.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:27.069
The waiting people always stand from tallest to shortest. That is the monotonic stack.

00:00:28.419 --> 00:00:31.740
Daily temperatures: how many days until a warmer day?

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:37.252
We push day indexes. The stack keeps temperatures decreasing from bottom to top.

00:00:37.702 --> 00:00:44.006
When seventy five arrives, it is warmer than seventy one and seventy three, so both pop and get their answers.

00:00:44.456 --> 00:00:48.473
Each day is pushed once and popped once, so the whole thing is linear.

00:00:49.823 --> 00:00:51.437
How to choose the direction.

00:00:51.887 --> 00:00:57.100
Want next greater? Keep the stack decreasing, and pop while the new item is bigger.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:02.182
Want next smaller? Keep it increasing, and pop while the new item is smaller.

00:01:02.632 --> 00:01:07.276
Store indexes, not values, so you can compute distances and widths.

00:01:08.626 --> 00:01:10.623
Where it shows up in interviews.

00:01:11.073 --> 00:01:15.253
Largest rectangle in a histogram and maximal rectangle in a matrix.

00:01:15.703 --> 00:01:19.998
Trapping rain water, where a pop finds a basin between two walls.

00:01:20.448 --> 00:01:23.954
Stock span and removing digits to make the smallest number.

00:01:25.304 --> 00:01:31.098
Keep a stack sorted, pop when the new item breaks the order, and the popped item has found its answer.
